From 121e6c37ab0528a91bd3311f29a244fe5fe4257e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude Opus 5.5" <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 15:55:53 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 6/9] libvncclient: a verified certificate for another name is
 asked about too

A certificate that verified against the system CA (or the CA file
given) but is not for the server's hostname was refused outright,
before GetX509CertFingerprintMismatchDecision could hear of it, while
one that did not verify reaches the hook whatever name it is for: its
hostname is never looked at. So a client that decides about
certificates itself was stricter with the certificate an authority
vouches for than with any self-signed one, and could not offer the
choice it offers for every other certificate, as TigerVNC's viewer
does for a name it did not expect.

With the hook installed, such a certificate now goes to the same
decision as the rest after the mismatch is logged: accepted at once
if its fingerprint is x509ExpectedFingerprint, and otherwise put to
the hook. Without the hook it is refused as before, now freeing the
certificate it parsed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/include/rfb/rfbclient.h b/include/rfb/rfbclient.h
index 1b22f3b..87027ad 100644
--- a/include/rfb/rfbclient.h
+++ b/include/rfb/rfbclient.h
@@ -223,10 +223,11 @@ typedef rfbCredential* (*GetCredentialProc)(struct _rfbClient* client, int crede
    fingerprint matches the one indicated in rfbCredential.x509Credential.x509ExpectedFingerprint,
    and whose answer decides whether the cert is accepted. That is every cert
    that could not be validated against the system CA (or the CA file given),
-   and, when this is set, every one that could and is for the server's
-   hostname as well: an authority vouching for a cert says who the server
-   claims to be, not that it is the one reached last time, so a client that
-   pins certificates has to see every one. Unset, a validated cert is accepted.
+   and, when this is set, every one that could as well, whether or not it is
+   for the server's hostname: an authority vouching for a cert says who the
+   server claims to be, not that it is the one reached last time, so a client
+   that pins certificates has to see every one. Unset, a validated cert is
+   accepted if it is for the hostname and refused if it is not.
  */
 typedef rfbBool (*GetX509CertFingerprintMismatchDecisionProc)(struct _rfbClient* client,
                                                               const char *remote_cert_subject,
diff --git a/src/libvncclient/tls_gnutls.c b/src/libvncclient/tls_gnutls.c
index 5534b53..ef0e969 100644
--- a/src/libvncclient/tls_gnutls.c
+++ b/src/libvncclient/tls_gnutls.c
@@ -229,7 +229,16 @@ verify_certificate_callback (gnutls_session_t session)
     {
       rfbClientLog("The certificate's owner does not match hostname '%s'\n",
               hostname);
-      return GNUTLS_E_CERTIFICATE_ERROR;
+      /* A client that decides about certificates itself is asked about this
+         one as well, as it is about a certificate that did not verify, whose
+         name was never looked at: refusing outright the one an authority
+         vouches for would be stricter with it than with any self-signed
+         certificate, whatever name that is for. */
+      if (!sptr->GetX509CertFingerprintMismatchDecision)
+        {
+          gnutls_x509_crt_deinit (cert);
+          return GNUTLS_E_CERTIFICATE_ERROR;
+        }
     }
 
   /* A client that decides about certificates itself hears of one that
